      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p>
          Against the <a href="/life-fitness-t3-treadmill/">Life Fitness T3</a>,
          the T7xe wins on raw spec sheet — a stronger motor, wider deck, and
          larger touchscreen — for roughly $1,000-$1,500 more. What the T3
          offers instead is the Life Fitness name and its decades of
          Equinox-and-hotel-gym brand pedigree, which matters to some buyers
          more than the spec gap; see our full
          <a href="/life-fitness/">Life Fitness brand guide</a>
          for the complete picture. Against the NordicTrack Commercial
          2950, the calculus flips: the NordicTrack costs roughly
          $1,000-$1,500 less and folds for storage, but trades away the AC
          motor's continuous-duty durability and leans on an iFit
          subscription to unlock most of its coaching features.
        </p>
        <p>
          One more comparison worth making mentally, even outside the table
          above: a genuinely budget-tier folding treadmill in the
          $600-$1,000 range. The gap in price looks enormous, and it is —
          but so is the gap in what you're actually buying. A budget
          machine's DC motor and lighter frame are built for occasional,
          moderate use, not the kind of daily-running duty cycle the T7xe is
          engineered to absorb without complaint. If your actual use pattern
          is closer to occasional walking, that price gap isn't buying you
          anything you'll use; if you're running most days of the week, it's
          buying you a machine that won't be the first thing to wear out.
        </p>

        <div class="not-prose bg-amber-50 border border-amber-200 rounded-2xl p-6 my-8">
